# Document Service Rules

## Layering & DTOs
- Never expose DTOs at the service layer. Services must accept and return domain objects only.
- Domain objects propagate up to the controller layer, where they are mapped to the appropriate response DTO (and vice versa for request DTOs).
- Mapping logic belongs in dedicated mapper classes/methods, not inline in controllers or services.

## Database
- Prefer PL/pgSQL functions for non-trivial data operations (aggregations, multi-step writes, complex filtering) instead of pushing logic into the Java layer.
- All schema changes and new functions go exclusively into Flyway migrations under `resources/database` — never modify the schema imperatively at runtime or outside migrations.
- Each migration must be additive/versioned per Flyway convention; never edit an already-applied migration.

## Controllers & REST Conventions
- Every controller endpoint must be secured with `@SecurityRequirement(name = "bearerAuth")`.
- Follow REST conventions strictly: resource paths use plural nouns, no verbs in URLs (e.g. `/documents`, not `/getDocuments`); the action is expressed through the HTTP method (GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE), not the path.
- Every controller method must include `io.swagger.v3.oas.annotations` annotations (`@Operation`, `@ApiResponse(s)`, `@Parameter` as applicable) to keep OpenAPI docs accurate.

## Testing
- Every unit of functionality must have unit tests written in Spock, with method names following the pattern `"should do X when Y"`.
- Unit tests must mock/stub collaborators and stay isolated from the database and Spring context where possible.
- The data access layer must have integration tests validating real behavior end-to-end (e.g. create then read returns expected data), run against Testcontainers-managed PostgreSQL with Flyway migrations applied automatically at startup.
- Integration tests must use a fresh, isolated database state per test class/run to avoid cross-test pollution.

## General
- Keep controllers thin: validation, security, and DTO mapping only — no business logic.
- Use constructor injection exclusively; avoid field injection.
- Wrap external/DB failures in meaningful domain exceptions, mapped to proper HTTP status codes via a centralized `@ControllerAdvice` exception handler.
- Log at service boundaries with contextual info (document IDs, operation) but never log sensitive payloads.